```c
irq = irq_of_parse_and_map(dpu_kms->pdev->dev.of_node, 0);
if (irq < 0) {
    DPU_ERROR("failed to get irq: %d\n", irq);
    return irq;
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code incorrectly checks if the return value of `irq_of_parse_and_map()` is less than 0 to determine if an error has occurred. However, this function returns 0 on failure, which means the condition `irq < 0` will never be true, leading to misleading error messages and improper handling of the IRQ initialization process.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!irq) {
    DPU_ERROR("failed to get irq\n");
    return -EINVAL;
}
```
Reason:
The fix changes the condition to check if `irq` is equal to 0 instead of less than 0. This correctly identifies the failure case when `irq_of_parse_and_map()` returns 0, allowing for proper error handling and logging, thus preventing misleading error messages.

